// Original revision questions. These are study aids, not past-paper questions.
const bank=[];const counts={};
const Q=(lesson,prompt,correct,wrong,explanation)=>{const n=counts[lesson]=(counts[lesson]||0)+1;bank.push({id:`${lesson}-q${n}`,lesson,prompt,options:[correct,...wrong],answer:0,explanation})};
Q('se-process','A team checks whether its attendance app prevents duplicate records, as specified. What is it checking?','Verification',['Whether the market wants any attendance app','Whether GitHub has a new release','Whether the team uses a particular process model'],'The duplicate rule is a specified requirement. Comparing implemented behavior with that rule is verification. Checking whether the app solves the intended user need is validation. A process model describes how work is arranged; it does not replace either check.');
Q('se-process','Which is the most checkable requirement?','Reject a second attendance record for the same student and class',['Make attendance excellent','Use a modern process','Provide a beautiful experience'],'The rejection rule identifies an input condition and an expected result. A tester can try to add the duplicate. Words such as excellent or beautiful need additional criteria before different people can evaluate them consistently.');
Q('se-process','What does a software process model primarily specify?','How development activities are arranged',['The exact value of every variable','A guarantee that no defects exist','Only the name of the programming language'],'A model arranges activities such as requirements, design, implementation, checks, and maintenance. It can use sequential phases or repeated cycles. Choosing a model does not guarantee correctness or determine every implementation detail.');
Q('se-process','The college changes an attendance policy after deployment. Which activity handles the required software change?','Maintenance',['Initial installation only','Deleting all requirements','Stopping every future test'],'Maintenance includes changes after delivery, including corrections and adaptations. The team must still understand the new requirement and check the changed behavior. Deployment does not end the software’s useful life.');
Q('se-models','A later release adds reports to an already usable attendance app. Which term best describes the added capability?','Increment',['Compiler optimization','Packet encapsulation','A Git merge conflict'],'An increment adds usable capability. An iteration revises a solution through a cycle. This release introduces reports that the earlier usable product did not provide. The two approaches can occur together, but the added capability is an increment.');
Q('se-models','A team revises the existing attendance screen after feedback. What is the most direct description?','An iteration improves the current solution',['A new network layer is created','All software history is discarded','No development work has occurred'],'The team repeats a development cycle to improve an existing solution. That is iteration. An increment emphasizes added product capability; this example focuses on improving the current screen.');
Q('se-models','Which description is accurate for a simplified Waterfall model?','Work is planned in sequential phases; changes can cause controlled rework',['Earlier requirements can never physically be changed','Testing is forbidden','Users must never provide requirements'],'Waterfall emphasizes a planned phase sequence. Returning to earlier work can be costly and require control, but it is possible. Saying that a phase can never be changed mistakes a model’s structure for a physical restriction.');
Q('se-models','Which statement about incremental and iterative development is correct?','A project can use both',['They always mean exactly the same thing','Incremental development cannot deliver usable software','Iteration removes the need for tests'],'A team can add reports in one release and improve the existing entry screen in another cycle. The first action adds capability; the second revises a solution. Tests remain necessary for both.');
Q('se-evolution','What is the defining emphasis of the spiral model?','Evaluating and reducing risks during repeated cycles',['Never examining requirements','Using a circular user interface','Deploying before every test'],'A spiral cycle identifies objectives, evaluates risks, performs appropriate development work, and plans the next cycle. Its risk emphasis distinguishes it from simply repeating a fixed list of phases.');
Q('se-evolution','Users cannot explain what a useful report should look like. What can a prototype help the team do?','Expose and clarify the reporting requirement',['Guarantee the final system has no bugs','Replace all production security checks','Automatically train a classifier'],'A sample report gives users something concrete to inspect. Their feedback can reveal a daily-view requirement that was hard to describe earlier. The prototype provides learning; it does not guarantee production quality.');
Q('se-evolution','What happens to a throwaway prototype after it has served its purpose?','It is discarded rather than becoming the production implementation',['It must always be deployed unchanged','It becomes a Git remote','It proves every requirement is complete'],'A throwaway prototype is built to answer a question. Its implementation is discarded after the team learns from it. An evolutionary prototype follows a different plan and is developed further with suitable quality work.');
Q('se-evolution','What does concurrent development permit?','Related activities can occupy different states at the same time',['All dependencies disappear','Every developer edits one file simultaneously','No change is ever reviewed'],'Requirements, design, and implementation work can overlap while remaining in identifiable states. Dependencies still matter. Concurrent work does not mean unrestricted simultaneous edits or the removal of reviews.');
Q('se-agile','Which action best uses an Agile feedback cycle?','Deliver a small useful change, inspect it with users, then revise priorities',['Ignore feedback until the entire product is finished','Avoid every written requirement','Choose tools instead of discussing user needs'],'A small usable result provides evidence. Users can check its actual behavior, and the team can adjust its next plan. Agile does not remove planning or documentation; it uses them alongside feedback and adaptation.');
Q('se-agile','Does valuing working software over comprehensive documentation mean documentation has no value?','No; useful documentation still has value',['Yes; every document must be deleted','Yes; requirements cannot be recorded','Only if Git is installed'],'The Agile value expresses a preference when priorities compete. It does not make the other item worthless. Teams still need documentation that supports shared understanding, maintenance, or required evidence.');
Q('se-agile','The user’s policy changes late in development. Which response is most consistent with Agile principles?','Discuss the effect and adapt the work while protecting quality',['Reject the change solely because it is late','Remove tests to save time automatically','Continue without telling the users'],'Agile welcomes changing requirements and uses collaboration to respond. A change still needs its impact assessed and its behavior checked. Adapting the plan is different from accepting every request without a quality decision.');
Q('se-agile','Which is stronger evidence of product progress?','A working attendance correction that users can inspect',['A large count of slides alone','The number of installed tools alone','A plan with no implemented behavior'],'Working behavior lets users evaluate whether the intended capability exists. Documents and tools can help the work, but their quantity alone does not demonstrate that the product solves the user’s problem.');
Q('se-scrum','Which event primarily examines how the team worked and how it can improve?','Sprint Retrospective',['Sprint Review','A Git push','JSON parsing'],'The Retrospective focuses on quality and team effectiveness. The Sprint Review focuses on the product outcome and possible adaptations. Confusing the two hides whether the question concerns the product or the working process.');
Q('se-scrum','Who is accountable for maximizing product value in Scrum?','Product Owner',['Only an external release manager','Only the person who writes most code','A mandatory project manager role'],'The Product Owner is accountable for maximizing product value and managing the Product Backlog effectively. Developers create the Increment, and the Scrum Master helps establish Scrum and improve effectiveness.');
Q('se-scrum','What is the official maximum Sprint length?','One month or less',['Exactly six months','At least three months','Always exactly one week'],'The Scrum Guide states that Sprints are one month or less. A college note may describe common two-to-four-week practice, but that example is not the complete official length rule.');
Q('se-scrum','Which statement about a usable Increment is correct?','It must meet the Definition of Done',['It only needs many completed tasks','It cannot be tested','It is the same as the entire Product Backlog'],'The Definition of Done states the required quality for an Increment. A task count alone does not establish usable product behavior. The Product Backlog lists product work; it is not the delivered Increment.');
Q('se-devops','A pipeline builds and tests changes, then waits for a human production approval. Which distinction applies?','It supports continuous delivery; production promotion is not fully automatic',['It necessarily performs continuous deployment','It cannot perform CI','It has no release process'],'Continuous delivery keeps verified changes ready for release and can retain an approval step. Continuous deployment also automates production promotion after the required gates. A build/test pipeline can provide CI in either arrangement.');
Q('se-devops','A blocking automated test fails. What should the described pipeline do?','Stop promotion and provide feedback for correction',['Promote anyway because the build exists','Delete all tests','Treat failure as a successful approval'],'A blocking check is a release condition. Failing that condition stops promotion. The team uses the result to correct the change; generating an artifact alone is not evidence that the checked requirement passed.');
Q('se-devops','Which statement describes DevOps most accurately?','Shared development/operations responsibility supported by feedback and automation',['A single tool installed on every machine','A replacement for all testing','Only a code-formatting rule'],'DevOps combines cooperation, shared responsibility, and practices across development and operations. Tools support these practices. Installing a tool alone does not create the required communication or operational feedback.');
Q('se-devops','Why monitor an application after release?','Live behavior can reveal issues that pre-release checks did not expose',['A successful build proves all live needs are satisfied','Monitoring automatically removes every defect','Only to count source files'],'Real traffic and environments can differ from test conditions. Monitoring supplies evidence about live behavior, which can guide the next change. It does not automatically solve every detected problem.');
Q('se-git-state','You stage file content A, then edit it to B without staging again. What does the next ordinary commit record for that file?','A',['B automatically','Both as separate commits automatically','Neither because a second edit is forbidden'],'git add selected content A for the index. Editing the working-tree file afterward does not replace that selection. The commit records A, and the unstaged difference toward B remains in the working tree.');
Q('se-git-state','Which command normally shows the difference selected for the next commit?','git diff --cached',['git push','git clone','git log --oneline only'],'git diff --cached compares the index with HEAD. That reveals staged changes. Ordinary git diff usually shows working-tree differences from the index. git log shows recorded history rather than the next selected snapshot.');
Q('se-git-state','Does git commit itself upload a commit to GitHub?','No; it records local history',['Yes; every repository always has GitHub access','Yes; staging uploads the content','Only when the file is Java'],'An ordinary commit records a local snapshot. Publishing to a configured remote uses push and depends on access and remote history. Git can be used without GitHub or even without a remote.');
Q('se-git-state','Which area contains the content selected for the next Git commit?','The staging area, also called the index',['Only the remote server','The browser DOM','The machine-learning validation set'],'The index stores the selected snapshot. The working tree can have additional edits, and the remote can have a different history. Keeping these states separate explains why staging and committing are distinct actions.');
Q('se-branches','What is a Git branch at the history level?','A movable reference to a commit',['A mandatory physical copy of every file','A separate GitHub account','A syntax rule for JavaScript'],'The branch name points to a commit. Making a commit on that branch advances the reference. Git can switch the working tree to another branch without creating a second physical project folder.');
Q('se-branches','main is at B and feature is at C, a direct descendant of B. main has no divergent commit. Which integration is possible?','A fast-forward to C',['A merge conflict is mandatory','Every old commit must be removed','A new network connection is always required'],'Because B is an ancestor of C, main can move its reference to C. No divergent history needs combining in this example. A policy can require a merge commit, but the ancestry permits fast-forward integration.');
Q('se-branches','Which action does git revert normally perform?','Create a new commit that reverses a chosen commit’s effect',['Erase the old commit from every repository','Delete the remote permanently','Automatically discard all unrelated files'],'Revert records an inverse change as new history. The original commit remains visible. This differs from moving a branch reference with reset, whose effects depend on its mode.');
Q('se-branches','Git reports a merge conflict in one file. What does this mean?','Git needs a decision to combine the conflicting edits',['Both complete histories are automatically lost','The branch name is invalid in every case','The file can never be repaired'],'A conflict identifies content Git could not combine automatically. Inspect the intended changes, edit the resolved result, stage it, and complete the merge. A conflict is not proof that the histories or file are irrecoverable.');
Q('se-github','Which operation downloads remote history without integrating it into the current branch?','git fetch',['git pull in every configuration','git commit','git add'],'Fetch retrieves objects and updates remote-tracking references. It does not itself integrate the fetched changes into the current branch. Pull combines fetching with a configured integration action, commonly merge or rebase.');
Q('se-github','What is a GitHub pull request?','A proposal to review and integrate changes',['Exactly the same operation as git pull','A local staging area','An automatic deletion of a branch'],'A pull request is a collaboration object on GitHub. People or automation can review it and later merge it. git pull is a Git command that fetches and integrates repository history.');
Q('se-github','What is the clearest distinction between a fork and a branch?','A fork is a hosted repository copy; a branch is a commit reference inside a repository',['Both always create a new user account','A branch contains no commits','A fork is only a file extension'],'A hosted fork gives another repository owner a copy for independent collaboration. A branch gives a name to a history within a repository. Both can support parallel work, but they operate at different levels.');
Q('se-github','Why might a remote reject an ordinary push?','The proposed update conflicts with the remote’s divergent history or access rules',['A local commit can never be uploaded','Every push must delete old commits','Git requires identical working-tree timestamps'],'A remote checks permissions and history rules. If other commits arrived remotely, the requested branch update may not be a permitted fast-forward. The rejection protects history; it does not mean that commits are inherently unpublishable.');

Q('wt-variables','What does this print?\nconst a = [1]; a.push(2); console.log(a.length);','2',['1','A required TypeError from push','undefined'],'const prevents reassignment of a. push changes the existing array, which is allowed. The array has two elements afterward. A declaration constraint on a binding does not freeze the object’s contents.');
Q('wt-variables','What happens here?\n{ let score = 8; }\nconsole.log(score);','ReferenceError when no outer score binding exists',['8','undefined in every case','The block becomes a Promise'],'The let binding exists only inside the block. The later statement cannot access it when no outer binding exists. var would follow a different scope rule; that does not change this let example.');
Q('wt-variables','What happens when a let binding is read in its scope before its declaration initializes it?','ReferenceError',['It always gives 0','It always gives undefined like a var read','The declaration is silently skipped'],'The binding is in its temporal dead zone until initialized. An early read throws ReferenceError. A var binding can have an earlier undefined value, but the let initialization rule is different.');
Q('wt-variables','Which reassignment is permitted?','let n = 1; n = 2;',['const n = 1; n = 2;','const n; n = 2;','const n = 1; const n = 2; in the same scope'],'let permits reassignment. const requires an initializer and prohibits reassignment. Redeclaring the same lexical binding in one scope is also invalid. These rules concern bindings rather than whether an object is mutable.');
Q('wt-operators','What are the results of "6" + 2 and "6" - 2?','"62" and 4',['8 and 4','"62" and "4"','8 and "62"'],'The + operation joins text because one operand is a string. Subtraction converts the numeric string to a number. Read each operator’s conversion behavior rather than assuming every operation uses the same rule.');
Q('wt-operators','Which expression is false?','"5" === 5',['Boolean([])','Boolean({})','5 === 5'],'Strict equality does not coerce the string to a number, so the different types make this comparison false. Empty arrays and objects are truthy. Comparing the number 5 with itself is true.');
Q('wt-operators','Why is [] === [] false?','The literals create two different array objects',['Empty arrays are falsy','Strict equality always rejects arrays','Both arrays have different lengths'],'Strict equality between objects checks whether they are the same reference. These literals produce separate arrays even though their contents match. A variable compared with itself can be strictly equal.');
Q('wt-operators','What is typeof null?','"object"',['"null"','"undefined"','"number"'],'JavaScript reports the historical typeof result "object" for null. null still represents an absent value and is not an ordinary object whose properties you can read. Memorize the result together with that limitation.');
Q('wt-functions','What does this print?\nconst twice = x => { x * 2; };\nconsole.log(twice(3));','undefined',['6','3','A mandatory SyntaxError'],'The arrow function has a block body and no return statement. Evaluating x * 2 does not automatically return it. An expression body, x => x * 2, would return 6 for this input.');
Q('wt-functions','A function receives an object reference and writes x.count = 2. Can the caller observe this change?','Yes, if it refers to that same object',['No, objects are always deeply copied','Only after JSON.stringify','Only when x is global'],'The argument value is a reference to the object. Both bindings can refer to that same object. A property mutation changes its contents, so the caller can observe the change through its own reference.');
Q('wt-functions','A function changes its parameter from object A to a new object B. Does that assignment redirect the caller’s binding?','No; it reassigns the local parameter binding',['Yes, every reference everywhere changes','Yes, but only for const callers','The original object must be deleted'],'Reassigning the parameter changes the value held by that local binding. The caller’s binding can continue pointing to A. This differs from mutating A’s properties through the original reference.');
Q('wt-functions','What is the result of an ordinary function call that executes no return value?','undefined',['null in every case','An empty Promise','The last expression in the block automatically'],'A normal JavaScript function without an executed return value returns undefined. The last expression is not automatically its result. Async functions have a different return contract: they return Promises.');
Q('wt-json','Which string contains valid JSON?','{"name":"Asha","active":true}',['{name:"Asha"}','{"name":"Asha",}','{"value":undefined}'],'JSON object keys use double quotes. JSON permits booleans but does not permit an undefined value or a trailing comma. The valid string can be parsed into an object.');
Q('wt-json','What does JSON.parse do?','Read JSON text and return a JavaScript value',['Always return a string unchanged','Convert every object into a DOM node','Execute arbitrary function definitions in JSON'],'Parsing reads the text under JSON syntax rules and constructs the represented value. That value can be an object, array, string, number, boolean, or null. Invalid syntax causes a SyntaxError.');
Q('wt-json','What is JSON.stringify({x: undefined, y: 2})?','{"y":2}',['{"x":undefined,"y":2}','{"x":null,"y":2}','A guaranteed empty string'],'An undefined property value is omitted when this ordinary object is serialized. The supported y property remains. Serialization rules differ for array entries, so do not apply this object-property result to every structure.');
Q('wt-json','Why is JSON stringify/parse not a universal deep-copy method?','Some values are omitted or changed, and circular references can cause failure',['It preserves every function and prototype exactly','JSON supports all JavaScript values','Parsing always returns the original reference'],'JSON represents a restricted data format. It does not preserve functions or object prototypes, and stringify can fail on a circular reference. It can copy suitable JSON-compatible data, but its limitations must match the input contract.');
Q('wt-callbacks','Is every callback asynchronous?','No; a callback can run synchronously or later',['Yes, a callback always creates a thread','Yes, forEach always waits one second','No callback can ever run later'],'Callback describes a passed function’s role. It does not specify timing. forEach calls its callback synchronously for this ordinary iteration, while a timer schedules a callback for later execution.');
Q('wt-callbacks','Which passes show as a callback without calling it in the argument expression?','values.forEach(show)',['values.forEach(show())','values.forEach(show(1))','values.forEach(undefined)'],'show is a function value. show() and show(1) call the function immediately and pass their return values. The receiving operation needs a callable function to invoke for the array elements.');
Q('wt-callbacks','What is the order?\n[1,2].forEach(x => console.log(x));\nconsole.log("end");','1, 2, end',['end, 1, 2','2, end, 1','end only'],'The callbacks for the ordinary dense array run during forEach. The statement after forEach runs when that iteration completes. No asynchronous operation is introduced in this example.');
Q('wt-callbacks','Does forEach automatically await Promises returned by async callbacks?','No',['Yes, it waits for every callback Promise','Yes, but only on arrays of numbers','It returns Promise.all automatically'],'forEach does not build an awaited result from callback returns. If work must complete before continuing, use an appropriate awaited loop or start independent work and await its combined Promises. The correct structure depends on the dependency contract.');
Q('wt-promises','What is the output order?\nconsole.log("A"); Promise.resolve().then(()=>console.log("B")); console.log("C");','A, C, B',['A, B, C','B, A, C','C, B, A'],'The two direct log statements run synchronously. The Promise reaction is queued and runs after the current synchronous work. A Promise that is already fulfilled still schedules its then reaction rather than running it inline.');
Q('wt-promises','Which are the three basic Promise states?','Pending, fulfilled, rejected',['Started, compiled, deployed','Waiting, staged, committed','Open, closed, forked'],'A Promise begins pending, then can settle as fulfilled or rejected. Settled refers to either final state. State changes do not restart the Promise after settlement.');
Q('wt-promises','When does the executor passed to new Promise run?','Synchronously while the Promise is constructed',['Only after every timer','Only when then is called','Never unless await appears'],'The Promise constructor calls its executor synchronously. Reactions attached with then run asynchronously. Separating executor timing from reaction timing is necessary for correct output prediction.');
Q('wt-promises','A Promise fulfills with 4, then resolve is called again with 8. What happens to its fulfillment value?','It remains 4',['It becomes 8','It becomes [4,8]','It returns to pending'],'A Promise cannot be settled again after fulfillment. The later attempt does not replace the established result. This makes the final result stable for its consumers.');
Q('wt-chains','What reaches the final handler?\nPromise.resolve(3).then(x => x * 2).then(x => console.log(x));','6',['3','undefined','A required rejection'],'The first handler returns 6. then creates a next Promise that fulfills with that return value, so the final handler receives 6. Omitting the return in a block-bodied handler would change the result.');
Q('wt-chains','A catch handler returns 9 normally. What can the following then receive?','The fulfilled value 9',['The original rejection must remain forever','A mandatory undefined value','No following handler can run'],'Returning normally from catch can recover the chain. The next Promise fulfills with the returned value. Throwing or returning a rejected Promise from catch would keep the continuation on a rejection path.');
Q('wt-chains','Promise.all receives [slowA, fastB]. Both fulfill. Which order does its result array use?','Input order: A result, then B result',['Completion order: B, then A','Random order','Only the last completed result'],'Promise.all preserves the input positions in its fulfillment array. Completion order does not reorder those results. It fulfills only after all the inputs fulfill.');
Q('wt-chains','An input to Promise.all rejects. Does Promise.all automatically cancel the other operations?','No',['Yes, it terminates every operation','Only if the array has two elements','All operations are rolled back'],'Promise.all rejects on an input rejection, but this does not cancel the underlying work of the other inputs. Cancellation needs separate support from the operations and the chosen control structure.');
Q('wt-async','What does an async function return to its caller?','A Promise',['Only an ordinary number','A callback without a result','Always undefined'],'An async function wraps its result in a Promise. An ordinary returned value fulfills it, while an unhandled thrown error rejects it. The caller must handle the Promise contract.');
Q('wt-async','What is the order?\nasync function f(){console.log("A"); await 0; console.log("B");}\nf(); console.log("C");','A, C, B',['A, B, C','C, A, B','B, C, A'],'The async function begins running immediately and logs A. await suspends its continuation even for this ordinary value. The caller logs C before the resumed function logs B.');
Q('wt-async','An awaited Promise rejects inside try. Which action can catch handle?','The rejection thrown at await',['Only a syntax error before the program loads','No error related to Promises','Only a DOM click'],'await converts the rejection into a thrown error at that point in the async function. A surrounding try/catch can handle it. Without suitable handling, the async function’s returned Promise can reject.');
Q('wt-async','Two independent operations should overlap. Which plan allows that?','Start both operations, then await their combined Promises',['Await the first before starting the second','Block the browser with an infinite loop','Replace both operations with forEach and assume it waits'],'Starting both operations before awaiting permits their underlying work to overlap when supported. Awaiting one before starting the next serializes those starts. Independence must be real; dependencies can require sequential work.');
Q('wt-arrays','What is [1,2,3].map(x=>x*2).filter(x=>x>2)?','[4,6]',['[2,4,6]','[2,3]','6'],'map first produces [2,4,6]. filter then retains values greater than 2, leaving [4,6]. Neither method here combines the elements into a single number.');
Q('wt-arrays','Which method is intended to combine array values into an accumulator result?','reduce',['filter','slice','forEach'],'reduce passes accumulated state through the visited elements. For a sum, provide an initial 0 and return the next sum from each callback. map transforms values and filter selects them.');
Q('wt-arrays','What does an ordinary forEach call return?','undefined',['An array of all callback returns','The last callback return','A Promise for all callbacks'],'forEach performs callback calls but does not collect a return array. Callback return values do not become its result. Use map when the intended result is an array of transformed values.');
Q('wt-arrays','Which sorts [10,2,1] numerically in ascending order?','a.sort((x,y)=>x-y)',['a.sort() with no comparator','a.filter(x=>x)','a.forEach(x=>x)'],'The numeric comparator compares the difference between values. The default sort compares string representations, which would order these values as 1,10,2. sort also mutates the original array.');
Q('wt-sets-maps','What is new Set([2,2,3]).size?','2',['3','1','undefined'],'The Set stores unique values. The repeated number 2 does not create another entry, so the entries are 2 and 3. Set uses size for the entry count.');
Q('wt-sets-maps','A Map receives set("a",1), then set("a",2). What are its size and value for a?','Size 1; value 2',['Size 2; value 1','Size 2; value [1,2]','Size 0; value undefined'],'The same primitive key identifies the existing association. The second set replaces its value without adding a second key. The Map therefore has one entry whose value is 2.');
Q('wt-sets-maps','Can a Map use an object as a key?','Yes; the object’s identity matters',['No; only strings are allowed','Yes; all objects with equal properties are the same key','Only after converting it to JSON'],'Maps can use object keys. The reference identifies the object, so two separate objects remain separate keys even if their properties match. JSON text would be a different, string-based key.');
Q('wt-sets-maps','Which is the correct entry-count property for Set and Map?','size',['length','count() in standard JavaScript','items'],'Set and Map expose size. Arrays use length for their index range. Applying array property expectations to these collections can produce undefined rather than the count.');
Q('wt-dom','What does querySelector return when there is no matching element?','null',['An empty array','A new element','undefined in every case'],'querySelector returns the first matching element or null. Check that result before writing properties when a match is not guaranteed. querySelectorAll has a different contract: a NodeList of all matches.');
Q('wt-dom','Which property should display user input as ordinary text?','textContent',['innerHTML for every user value','outerHTML to execute the text','A script tag automatically'],'textContent writes text without interpreting it as markup. innerHTML parses HTML, which changes the meaning and can create security problems for untrusted input. The property choice should match the intended text-only contract.');
Q('wt-dom','Is the NodeList from querySelectorAll automatically updated when later matching nodes are added?','No; it is a static NodeList',['Yes; it is always live','Only if the nodes are paragraphs','It updates the original HTML file on disk'],'querySelectorAll takes a static selection of matches. Later matching elements do not automatically join that list. Other DOM collection APIs can return live collections, so identify the particular API before tracing behavior.');
Q('wt-dom','JavaScript changes a paragraph’s textContent in the browser. What has changed?','The live document node',['The source HTML file on disk automatically','The entire Git commit history','The network routing table'],'The selected DOM node represents the currently loaded document. Changing its text updates the page. The browser does not automatically write that edit back into the original source file.');
Q('wt-events','Which registers a callback correctly?','button.addEventListener("click", handle)',['button.addEventListener("click", handle())','button.addEventListener("click", null())','button.addEventListener(handle(), "click")'],'Pass the function value handle. Calling handle() during registration passes its result instead, unless that result is intentionally a handler. The event type belongs in the first argument.');
Q('wt-events','What does event.currentTarget identify?','The node whose listener is currently running',['Always the original clicked descendant','The next network router','The HTML file name'],'currentTarget identifies the listener’s node for the current callback. target identifies the original event target. These can differ when a bubbling event is handled by an ancestor.');
Q('wt-events','What does preventDefault do for a cancelable event?','Cancel its default action',['Always stop all propagation','Remove every listener','Delete the target node'],'preventDefault requests cancellation of a cancelable default action. stopPropagation affects travel along the event path. One action does not automatically perform the other.');
Q('wt-events','A listener increments one captured count from 0 on each click. What is count after three clicks?','3',['1','0','A new independent value for each call'],'The listener keeps access to the same captured binding. Each call adds one to its current value, giving 1, then 2, then 3. Registering the listener once does not reset the count for each event.');

Q('cn-osi','Which OSI layer is primarily responsible for logical addressing and routing?','Network',['Presentation','Physical','Session'],'Routing concerns forwarding toward a logical network destination. That is primarily the Network Layer responsibility. The Data Link Layer handles delivery across an individual link, and the Physical Layer carries signals.');
Q('cn-osi','Which is the correct top-to-bottom OSI order?','Application, Presentation, Session, Transport, Network, Data Link, Physical',['Application, Session, Presentation, Network, Transport, Physical, Data Link','Physical, Network, Data Link, Transport, Session, Presentation, Application','Application, Transport, Presentation, Session, Data Link, Network, Physical'],'Read downward from the application’s needs to transmitted signals. Presentation handles representation; Session handles dialogue; Transport handles end-to-end transport; Network routes; Data Link frames local delivery; Physical transmits signals.');
Q('cn-osi','An ordinary Ethernet switch forwarding frames by MAC information is primarily associated with which layer?','Data Link',['Session','Presentation','Application'],'The described forwarding uses link-layer frames and MAC information. That maps primarily to Layer 2. A real switch can have additional functions, so the stated behavior matters more than a device name alone.');
Q('cn-osi','Which statement about OSI is accurate?','It is a reference model for separating responsibilities',['Every real protocol maps perfectly to exactly one layer','It is a mandatory cable connector','It guarantees delivery of every packet'],'OSI provides a model for reasoning about responsibilities. Real protocols and products can combine or cross responsibilities. The model itself does not guarantee delivery or prescribe one physical connector.');
Q('cn-encapsulation','A router forwards a packet onto another link. What normally happens to the link frame?','The router constructs a new frame for the outgoing link',['The incoming frame must remain identical forever','The router removes every network address permanently','Only the application layer can forward it'],'A frame supports one local-link delivery. The router processes the incoming frame, forwards the packet, and constructs suitable outgoing link information. The next-hop MAC destination can therefore differ between links.');
Q('cn-encapsulation','Host A reaches Host B through a router. On the first Ethernet link, which destination normally belongs in the frame?','The next-hop router interface’s MAC address',['Always Host B’s remote MAC address','The user’s email address','The application function name'],'The first frame must reach the next device on the local link. The network packet carries the destination information used for routing toward Host B. Link and network addressing solve different delivery tasks.');
Q('cn-encapsulation','Which device behavior is primarily Physical Layer work?','Repeating a signal',['Selecting a route using an IP destination','Parsing JSON','Reviewing a Git commit'],'Repeating a signal concerns physical transmission. Routing uses network-layer information. A multifunction device can do more than one thing, but the described signal-handling responsibility is Physical Layer work.');
Q('cn-encapsulation','Must a forwarded IP packet remain byte-for-byte unchanged at a router?','No; fields such as TTL can change',['Yes, no field can ever change','Only if it is inside Ethernet','A router can never inspect a packet'],'A router can update forwarding-related fields such as IPv4 TTL. Translation can also change addresses. Saying that a packet continues toward the same destination does not mean every byte remains unchanged.');
Q('cn-media','Which mode allows simultaneous transmission in both directions?','Full duplex',['Half duplex','Simplex','A fixed JSON mode'],'Full duplex supports both directions at the same time. Half duplex supports both directions at different times. Simplex supports only one direction. None of these labels by itself identifies the physical cable.');
Q('cn-media','Which is a guided medium?','Optical fiber',['Free-space radio propagation','A wireless broadcast path','The OSI Session Layer'],'Optical fiber provides a physical guiding path for light. Radio propagation through free space is unguided. A protocol layer is a responsibility category, not a transmission medium.');
Q('cn-media','A push-to-talk link allows each side to transmit, but only one at a time. Which mode is described?','Half duplex',['Simplex','Full duplex','No possible communication mode'],'Both directions are possible, so it is not simplex. They cannot operate simultaneously in this described link, so it is half duplex rather than full duplex.');
Q('cn-media','Does full duplex imply unlimited bit rate?','No; each direction still has capacity constraints',['Yes, every cable becomes infinitely fast','Only for optical fiber','It means a packet needs no frame'],'Duplex describes the direction and timing of communication. Capacity depends on the medium and system. Simultaneous transmission does not remove bandwidth, noise, encoding, or equipment limits.');
Q('cn-multiplex','Which resource separates channels in FDM?','Frequency bands',['Only time slots','Git branch names','Array indices in JSON'],'Frequency-division multiplexing assigns separated frequency bands. Time-division multiplexing assigns slots, while wavelength-division multiplexing assigns optical wavelengths. Identify the actual separation resource in the question.');
Q('cn-multiplex','In synchronous TDM, what can happen to a fixed slot when its source has nothing to send?','The slot can remain allocated and unused',['It must become a different frequency band','All other sources must stop forever','The receiver automatically invents data'],'A fixed-slot schedule preserves each source’s position. An idle source can therefore leave its slot unused. Statistical TDM can assign capacity to active sources, but that is a different scheduling arrangement.');
Q('cn-multiplex','Which method separates optical signals by wavelength?','WDM',['Stop-and-Wait','JSON.parse','CSMA/CD'],'Wavelength-division multiplexing uses different optical wavelengths on a shared path. Stop-and-Wait is an acknowledgment/retry protocol, while CSMA/CD concerns shared-medium access. They do not name optical wavelength separation.');
Q('cn-multiplex','A fixed TDM frame contains A, B, C slots. How does the receiver identify B’s data in this example?','By its agreed slot position',['By whichever item finishes last','By reading a Git hash','By dropping the second slot always'],'The sender and receiver share a fixed-slot schedule. The B position identifies B’s stream. This example does not need completion-order guessing because the positions already provide the separation rule.');
Q('cn-signals','A system sends 1000 symbols/s and each symbol represents 2 bits. What is its stated bit rate?','2000 bits/s',['500 bits/s','1000 bits/s','1000 hertz in every interpretation'],'Multiply symbol rate by represented bits per symbol for this stated mapping. The result is 2000 bits/s. Keep symbol count, bit count, and frequency bandwidth as different quantities.');
Q('cn-signals','For B = 3000 Hz and linear S/N = 15, what is B log2(1 + S/N)?','12000 bits/s',['45000 bits/s','3000 bits/s','15 bits/s'],'1 + S/N is 16, and log2(16) is 4. Multiplying by 3000 gives 12000 bits/s. This is the Shannon-model capacity under the given assumptions, not a measured application throughput.');
Q('cn-signals','An SNR is supplied as 20 dB. What must happen before using it as S/N in Shannon’s formula?','Convert it to a linear power ratio of 100',['Insert 20 unchanged as the ratio','Divide bandwidth by 20','Replace log2 with a string comparison'],'The power-ratio conversion is S/N = 10^(dB/10). For 20 dB, this is 10^2 = 100. The capacity formula requires that linear ratio, not the decibel number.');
Q('cn-signals','Under the ideal-noiseless textbook Nyquist model, B = 1000 Hz and M = 4. What is 2B log2(M)?','4000 bits/s',['2000 bits/s','8000 bits/s','4000 symbols/s as the required bit-rate answer'],'log2(4) is 2 bits per distinguishable symbol in the stated model. Multiplying 2 × 1000 × 2 gives 4000 bits/s. This calculation has different assumptions from the noisy-channel Shannon bound.');
Q('cn-framing','With a 0 inserted after five consecutive data 1 bits, what is the stuffed form of 111111?','1111101',['1111110','0111111','1111011'],'Send the first five 1 bits, insert 0, then send the remaining data 1. The inserted 0 breaks the run. The receiver removes it using the same agreed stuffing rule.');
Q('cn-framing','What is the main purpose of payload stuffing?','Prevent payload patterns from being confused with frame boundaries',['Correct every damaged bit','Increase the number of usable application bytes without overhead','Select the shortest network route'],'Stuffing protects the boundary interpretation. It adds overhead to encode special payload patterns safely. Detection or correction of corruption needs a separate check or code; stuffing alone does not provide those guarantees.');
Q('cn-framing','Should the sender apply the payload bit-stuffing rule to the boundary flag itself?','No; the boundary flag is transmitted as the protocol defines it',['Yes, every flag must be indistinguishable from data','Only if the frame has no payload','The flag is always deleted'],'The receiver must recognize the flag. Stuffing transforms payload patterns so they do not imitate that flag. Applying the same transformation indiscriminately to the boundary would defeat the framing rule.');
Q('cn-framing','What does the receiver do with a correctly recognized stuffed bit?','Remove it while reconstructing payload data',['Deliver it as an extra application bit','Treat every following bit as a new frame','Change the IP route'],'Stuffing is reversible encoding for framing. The receiver removes the inserted redundancy and reconstructs the original data. Keeping the extra bit would change the payload the application receives.');
Q('cn-errors','Even parity is used. Which corruption can it fail to detect?','Two flipped bits',['Exactly one flipped bit','Exactly three flipped bits','Every odd number of flips'],'Two flips preserve whether the total count of 1 bits is even. Any odd number changes that parity and is detected. Parity does not identify and correct the flipped positions.');
Q('cn-errors','Which operation replaces subtraction in binary CRC division?','XOR',['Ordinary decimal subtraction with borrowing','Multiplication of ASCII codes','JSON parsing'],'CRC polynomial arithmetic uses binary coefficients. XOR combines them without carries or borrows. The generator and appended-zero convention determine the specific division calculation.');
Q('cn-errors','A CRC generator has degree 3. How many zeros are appended to the data before generating the remainder?','3',['1','4','The number of data bits always'],'The remainder has room for at most three lower-degree coefficients. Append three zeros, divide by the generator, and use the resulting remainder as check bits. The degree is one less than the generator’s bit length.');
Q('cn-errors','A received CRC remainder is zero. Which conclusion is justified?','The check found no detected error',['Every bit is guaranteed correct under every possible corruption','The receiver has corrected all bits','The frame cannot contain payload data'],'Zero means the received codeword passes that check. Some error patterns can preserve the code relation and remain undetected. The result is therefore evidence within the code’s guarantees, not universal proof of correctness.');
Q('cn-hamming','For 4 data bits, what is the smallest r satisfying 2^r ≥ 4 + r + 1?','3',['1','2','4'],'At r = 2, 4 is smaller than 7. At r = 3, 8 equals 8, so the inequality first holds. The standard arrangement therefore uses four data positions and three check positions.');
Q('cn-hamming','Under an ordinary Hamming code’s single-bit error assumption, what can a nonzero syndrome identify?','The flipped bit position',['The best application protocol','Every possible multi-bit error with guaranteed correction','A JSON object key'],'The parity-check outcomes match a position’s check pattern when exactly one bit is wrong. That identifies the position to flip. Multiple errors can produce an ambiguous or misleading syndrome, so the assumption matters.');
Q('cn-hamming','Which statement about ordinary and extended Hamming codes is accurate?','Extended Hamming adds overall parity for SECDED behavior',['Ordinary distance-3 Hamming guarantees correction of every two-bit error','They have no check bits','Extended Hamming removes all parity'],'Ordinary distance-3 Hamming corrects one bit. Adding overall parity produces the usual extended code with single-error correction and double-error detection. Detection and correction are different guarantees.');
Q('cn-hamming','In a common Hamming(7,4) arrangement, which positions hold parity bits?','1, 2, 4',['3, 5, 7','1, 3, 5, 7','Every position'],'Powers-of-two positions hold the parity bits in the common indexing convention. The remaining positions carry the four data bits. State the convention before constructing or checking the complete bit pattern.');
Q('cn-stopwait','An ACK is lost after the receiver delivers frame 0. The sender retries frame 0. What should the receiver do?','Recognize the duplicate, acknowledge it, and avoid redelivery',['Deliver the same payload again without checking','Assume frame 1 has arrived','Reset all application data'],'The receiver already accepted frame 0 and expects the next sequence number. The repeated frame 0 is a duplicate. Acknowledging it can allow the sender to recover without delivering the same application data twice.');
Q('cn-stopwait','What does a sender timeout prove?','The expected acknowledgment did not arrive in time',['The data frame was definitely lost','The receiver definitely crashed','Every earlier frame failed'],'Silence does not distinguish a lost frame, a lost acknowledgment, or excessive delay. The sender can retry according to the protocol. Sequence numbers make that retry safe against duplicate delivery in the stated model.');
Q('cn-stopwait','How many frames are outstanding in the basic Stop-and-Wait scheme?','One',['An unlimited number','Exactly four','The full sequence-number space'],'The sender waits for acknowledgment of one frame before moving to the next. This limits receiver pressure but can leave the link idle while a response travels back. Sliding-window protocols permit more outstanding work.');
Q('cn-stopwait','Which distinction is correct?','Flow control limits outstanding data; error control handles loss or corruption',['Flow control always encrypts data','Error control means changing the application’s font','Both refer only to a cable connector'],'Flow control manages the receiver’s ability to accept data. Error control provides mechanisms for lost or damaged transmissions. A protocol can combine both responsibilities, but the purposes remain distinct.');
Q('cn-windows','Frames 0–3 are sent and frame 1 is lost. Under standard Go-Back-N, what is resent after the relevant timeout?','1, 2, 3',['Only 0','Only 3','No frame can be resent'],'The standard Go-Back-N receiver discards later out-of-order frames. Recovery goes back to the missing frame and resends the outstanding suffix. Selective Repeat uses different buffering and retransmission rules.');
Q('cn-windows','For the same loss, what can Selective Repeat do with correctly received frames 2 and 3?','Buffer them and resend only missing frame 1',['Always discard them like standard Go-Back-N','Deliver a missing frame 1 without receiving it','Disable sequence numbers'],'Selective Repeat retains acceptable out-of-order frames. It can request or await retransmission of the missing frame without repeating those saved frames. This requires receiver state and an adequate sequence-number space.');
Q('cn-windows','With 3-bit sequence numbers and equal Selective Repeat windows, what is the usual maximum window size?','4',['8','7','16'],'There are eight sequence values. The textbook equal-window limit uses at most half the space to avoid confusing old and new frames, so 2^(3−1) = 4. Go-Back-N uses a different bound.');
Q('cn-windows','With 3-bit sequence numbers, what is the usual maximum Go-Back-N sender window?','7',['8','4','16'],'The usual sender limit is 2^k − 1. For k = 3, that is 7. Reserving space prevents sequence ambiguity in the standard protocol model. The Selective Repeat half-space rule is different.');
Q('cn-access','Why can CSMA stations collide even after both sense idle?','Each station can start before the other signal reaches it',['Sensing always encrypts the signal','An idle channel cannot transmit data','The MAC addresses must be identical'],'Propagation takes time. Each station can observe idle before hearing the other’s new transmission. Carrier sensing reduces collision probability but cannot provide instantaneous knowledge of every station.');
Q('cn-access','Which environment uses CSMA/CD in the textbook model?','Shared half-duplex Ethernet',['Ordinary switched full-duplex Ethernet transmission','Every Wi-Fi transmission','A JSON parser'],'CSMA/CD applies to suitable shared half-duplex Ethernet, where simultaneous transmissions can collide and be detected. Modern switched full-duplex links do not use this collision-detection access procedure for ordinary frame transmission.');
Q('cn-access','Under ideal textbook assumptions, which maximum throughput is larger?','Slotted ALOHA at about 36.8%',['Pure ALOHA at about 50%','Both are always 100%','Pure ALOHA at about 73.6%'],'The ideal maxima are 1/e for slotted ALOHA and 1/(2e) for pure ALOHA. Slot boundaries reduce the vulnerable overlap interval. These model maxima do not guarantee a particular real deployment’s throughput.');
Q('cn-access','What does CSMA/CA attempt to do?','Reduce collisions using avoidance techniques',['Guarantee that collisions are impossible','Use the same collision-detection behavior as all shared wired links','Remove the need for acknowledgment in every wireless protocol'],'Avoidance uses sensing and waiting/backoff, with protocol features such as acknowledgments and optional RTS/CTS. Hidden stations and other limitations can still cause failure. The name does not imply a guarantee of no collisions.');

Q('ai-tasks','A model predicts pass or fail, stored as 0 or 1. What task is described?','Classification',['Regression merely because labels are numbers','Unsupervised learning in every case','A communication-channel code'],'The values represent categories. Numeric encoding does not change the target’s meaning. Predicting a numerical score would instead be regression; predicting its pass/fail category is classification.');
Q('ai-tasks','Which data should fit a normalization transformation before evaluation?','Training data only',['The complete training, validation, and test data together','Test targets only','Whichever set gives the highest final score'],'A data-dependent transformation can learn information from its inputs. Fitting it on test data leaks evaluation information into preparation. Fit it on training data, then apply the learned transformation to the other sets.');
Q('ai-tasks','What is a feature?','An input used to produce a prediction',['Always the correct output label','The test accuracy percentage','A GitHub pull request'],'A feature describes the input, such as hours studied. The target describes the known outcome, such as the actual score. Keeping input and target roles separate prevents accidental use of answer information during prediction.');
Q('ai-tasks','What is the main role of a final separate test set?','Evaluate the selected model on data not used for fitting or repeated selection',['Train weights every epoch','Select every hyperparameter repeatedly','Replace all training data'],'The final test set provides a later evaluation of the selected model. Repeatedly choosing model settings from its results turns it into selection data and weakens the independence of that evaluation.');
Q('ai-regression','For y-hat = 2x + 3 and x = 4, what is the prediction?','11',['8','7','14'],'Multiply the input 4 by the weight 2 to obtain 8, then add bias 3. The prediction is 11. The actual target would be needed separately to calculate a prediction error.');
Q('ai-regression','Actual values are [10,20,30], predictions are [8,22,29]. What is MSE?','3',['1/3','9','0'],'The residuals are 2, -2, and 1. Their squares are 4, 4, and 1, totaling 9. Divide by three examples to get MSE 3. Signed residual cancellation is not the MSE operation.');
Q('ai-regression','Why square residuals in MSE?','To prevent sign cancellation and penalize larger residuals more strongly',['To turn every regression into classification','To make every loss equal to accuracy','To remove the need for targets'],'Positive and negative residuals can cancel if directly averaged. Squaring makes their contributions nonnegative and gives large magnitudes greater influence. Targets are still needed to calculate those residuals.');
Q('ai-regression','If a target is measured in rupees, what are MSE’s units?','Squared rupees',['A dimensionless accuracy percentage','Always seconds','Rupees with no squaring'],'Each residual has target units. Squaring it gives squared target units, and averaging preserves those units. MAE would retain the original target units, while RMSE takes the square root to restore them.');
Q('ai-classification','Why is logistic regression normally a classification method?','It transforms a linear score into a probability used for a class decision',['Its name guarantees a continuous-value target','It has no learned parameters','It only predicts network bandwidth'],'The usual binary logistic model applies sigmoid to a linear score. A threshold then selects a category. The method’s historical name does not determine the target meaning; the output contract does.');
Q('ai-classification','P(spam) is 0.70 and the rule is spam when probability ≥ 0.50. What is predicted?','Spam',['Not spam','A guaranteed true label with no uncertainty','No class can be chosen'],'The estimated probability meets the chosen threshold. The model therefore predicts spam. A prediction is not proof of correctness; the true label is needed to evaluate whether the decision was right.');
Q('ai-classification','Which output arrangement is typical for several mutually exclusive classes?','Softmax over the class scores',['One unrestricted regression number with no decision rule','A required Set object','A Hamming parity bit'],'Softmax normalizes the class-score vector into values that sum to one. Selecting a class then uses a decision rule such as the largest value. Independent multilabel decisions often use separate sigmoid outputs instead.');
Q('ai-classification','Can changing a binary classification threshold change its predicted classes?','Yes, even when the estimated probabilities stay the same',['No, a probability fixes a class independently of the threshold','Only if the training data is deleted','Only for a Java program'],'The threshold is part of the decision rule. A probability 0.60 satisfies threshold 0.50 but not threshold 0.70. Adjusting the decision rule can change false-positive and false-negative counts without retraining the model.');
Q('ai-neuron','For x = [2,3], w = [0.5,-1], b = 1, what is z?','-1',['0','1','-2'],'The contributions are 2 × 0.5 = 1 and 3 × (-1) = -3. Their sum is -2. Adding bias 1 produces z = -1. Activation is a separate next step.');
Q('ai-neuron','If z = -1 and the activation is ReLU, what is the neuron output?','0',['-1','1','A probability of exactly 0.5'],'ReLU is max(0,z). For a negative z, it returns zero. A sigmoid would have a different result; identify the specified activation before calculating the output.');
Q('ai-neuron','What is the role of a neuron’s bias?','Add a learned offset to the weighted sum',['Always count the training examples','Replace all input features','Guarantee the prediction is correct'],'The bias shifts the pre-activation value. It allows an offset independent of the current input contributions. It is a model parameter, but no single bias value guarantees correctness across examples.');
Q('ai-neuron','Which order matches the stated neuron formula?','Multiply inputs by weights, sum, add bias, apply activation',['Apply activation to the class label, then delete inputs','Add the learning rate to every input and stop','Count epochs before doing any multiplication'],'The pre-activation sum combines weighted inputs and bias. The activation transforms that combined value. Applying the activation separately to each raw input would define a different calculation.');
Q('ai-activations','What is ReLU applied to [-2,0,3]?','[0,0,3]',['[-2,0,3]','[2,0,3]','[0.5,0.5,0.5]'],'ReLU keeps each positive input and maps each negative input to zero. At zero it outputs zero. It is not absolute value, so -2 does not become 2.');
Q('ai-activations','Why use nonlinear hidden activations?','Stacked linear transformations alone still represent a linear mapping',['They guarantee perfect accuracy','They remove all learned weights','They convert every task into JSON'],'Without nonlinear operations, composing linear or affine layers produces another linear or affine mapping. Nonlinear activations enlarge the relationships the network can represent. This increases expressive capacity but does not guarantee generalization.');
Q('ai-activations','What does softmax produce for two equal scores?','[0.5,0.5]',['[1,1]','[0,0]','The unchanged score values in every case'],'Equal exponentiated scores contribute equally to the normalization. Each receives half the total, and the outputs sum to one. The result does not mean both mutually exclusive classes are simultaneously the true class.');
Q('ai-activations','Which output is common for an unrestricted numerical regression target?','A linear output',['A required softmax over two classes','A fixed string','A CRC remainder'],'A linear output can represent unrestricted numerical values. Bounded or constrained regression tasks may need another output contract. Softmax expresses a different, mutually exclusive class-score normalization.');
Q('ai-ann','A Dense layer has 3 input features, 4 units, and biases. How many parameters are there?','16',['12','4','7'],'Each of four units has three input weights, giving 12 weights. Each also has one bias, giving four more parameters. The total is 16.');
Q('ai-ann','Does increasing the batch size multiply a Dense layer’s learned parameter count?','No; examples reuse the same weights and biases',['Yes, every example permanently adds new weights','Only for a batch of two','The batch size is always the number of classes'],'The batch contains more examples, not more model units. Each example uses the same parameter values. Intermediate tensor sizes can change with batch size while the learned parameter count remains fixed.');
Q('ai-ann','Does a basic Flatten layer itself learn Dense weights?','No; it changes the shape',['Yes, it creates one weight for every pixel automatically','Only when input values are zero','It learns the entire test set'],'Flatten rearranges the shape of its input into a vector. The subsequent Dense layer has its own weights. Do not assign Dense parameter counts to a shape-only operation.');
Q('ai-ann','Does a larger parameter count guarantee better performance on unseen data?','No',['Yes, regardless of data and training','Only if all biases equal zero','The question has no relationship to models'],'More parameters can increase representational capacity. They can also fit training-specific patterns and require suitable data and training. Evaluate generalization on separate data rather than assuming size determines quality.');
Q('ai-training','w = 2, gradient = 3, learning rate = 0.1. What is one gradient-descent update?','1.7',['2.3','0.6','3.1'],'Gradient descent subtracts the learning-rate-scaled gradient. Compute 2 - 0.1 × 3 = 1.7. Adding the gradient would move in the opposite local direction from this descent rule.');
Q('ai-training','Which operation primarily computes network gradients using the chain rule?','Backpropagation',['Only a Git push','JSON.stringify','Prediction without any differentiation'],'Backpropagation calculates derivatives through the network. An optimizer then uses those derivatives in an update rule. Measuring loss, calculating gradients, and changing parameters are related but distinct actions.');
Q('ai-training','What is an epoch?','One pass through the training examples',['Always one parameter update','One class label','One test example in every model'],'An epoch covers the training data once. With mini-batches, that pass can include many updates. Batch size and training-set size determine how examples are grouped during the pass.');
Q('ai-training','Can an excessively large learning rate cause overshooting?','Yes',['No, every larger value is always better','Only if the model has no parameters','The learning rate is the true class label'],'An update scaled too strongly can move past a useful region and cause unstable behavior. A local descent direction does not guarantee that an arbitrarily large step will reduce loss.');
Q('ai-tensorflow','Which Keras method performs training updates on supplied examples?','fit',['compile','predict','summary'],'fit runs the training procedure using data and targets. compile configures its loss, optimizer, and metrics. predict produces outputs without the ordinary training update loop.');
Q('ai-tensorflow','What does compile primarily configure?','Optimizer, loss and metrics',['All training examples are automatically downloaded','The final model is guaranteed correct','A mandatory GitHub repository'],'compile sets how the model should be trained and evaluated. It does not itself perform the full fit process on examples. Data and an appropriate training call are still needed.');
Q('ai-tensorflow','What is GradientTape used for?','Recording suitable operations for automatic differentiation',['Buffering Ethernet frames','Selecting a Git branch','Parsing the DOM'],'GradientTape tracks operations involved in a calculation so gradients can be requested. An optimizer can use those gradients afterward. Recording and applying an update are separate steps.');
Q('ai-tensorflow','A model has one linear numerical output and uses MSE. Which task is this example configured for?','Regression',['Mutually exclusive ten-class classification','JSON serialization','Stop-and-Wait acknowledgment'],'The output represents one unrestricted numerical prediction, and MSE measures its numerical error against a target. A ten-class classifier would need an appropriate class-output contract and matching loss.');
Q('ai-evaluation','A dataset has 90 negative and 10 positive examples. Always predicting negative gives what accuracy and positive recall?','90% accuracy, 0 recall',['100% accuracy, 1 recall','10% accuracy, 1 recall','0% accuracy, 0.9 recall'],'Ninety negative predictions are correct, so accuracy is 90/100. None of the ten positives is found, so recall is 0/10. The common class hides the complete positive-class failure in the overall accuracy.');
Q('ai-evaluation','TP = 8 and FP = 2. What is precision?','0.8',['0.2','4','0.1'],'Precision asks what fraction of predicted positives are actually positive. Its denominator is TP + FP = 10, so the value is 8/10 = 0.8. Recall uses actual positives and a different denominator.');
Q('ai-evaluation','TP = 8 and FN = 2. What is recall?','0.8',['0.2','4','It cannot be calculated with these values'],'Recall asks what fraction of actual positives were found. Its denominator is TP + FN = 10, giving 8/10 = 0.8. Precision instead counts false positives in its denominator.');
Q('ai-evaluation','Training performance improves while validation performance becomes worse. What concern does this raise?','Overfitting to training-specific patterns',['Guaranteed improvement on unseen examples','A required network cable replacement','Proof that every label is correct'],'The divergence suggests that the model is learning patterns that do not transfer well. Investigate data, capacity, and training choices. The observation raises a generalization concern; training improvement alone cannot dismiss it.');

export const questions = bank.map((q,i)=>{
  const offset=(i*3+1)%4;
  return {...q,options:q.options.slice(offset).concat(q.options.slice(0,offset)),answer:(4-offset)%4,subject:q.lesson.split('-')[0].toUpperCase()};
});
